An explanation:

• The highest threat is Field_2 (0.15) which already has 4 drones protecting it – so we keep Drone_1, Drone_4, Drone_5 and Drone_8 there.
• Next, Field_3 (threat 0.14) is unprotected and requires 2 drones. Drone_6 is idle and lies right within Field_3’s boundaries; to complete the pair, we choose the Field_4 drone that is closest to Field_3 – Drone_7 (its location, (41,24), is only 8 units vertically away from Field_3’s top edge at y = 32). We thus reassign Drone_7 from Field_4 to Field_3.
• Field_4 (threat 0.12) originally had 3 drones protecting it. After reassigning Drone_7 to Field_3, only Drone_2 and Drone_3 remain. Since partial protection is not very effective, we accept that Field_4 will now be unprotected (or only partially protected) because protecting the higher‐threat Field_3 fully is our priority.
• Field_1, with the lowest threat (0.11), is left without any drone protection.

This grouping follows the instructions: always keep full protection when it already exists and, if needed, reassign the drone nearest to a higher‐threat unprotected field (here Field_3) even if that sacrifices full protection of a lower-threat field (Field_4).
