We have only 8 drones available while the three fields with higher bird‐threat (Field_3 with threat 0.17, Field_1 with threat 0.12, and Field_4 with threat 0.10) would require 2, 4, and 3 drones respectively—a total of 9—to be fully covered. Because full coverage is crucial (partial protection does little to stop the birds), we must prioritize. Field_3 is the highest threat and already has one drone protecting (Drone_6) and one en route (Drone_7), so we keep those on Field_3. 

Next, Field_1 (threat 0.12) is more important than Field_4 (threat 0.10) and currently has no drones. To quickly secure this field we reassign the four drones originally on Field_2 (which is low‐priority, threat 0.04) to Field_1. Choosing those drones is also justified by looking at their locations – although they were originally covering Field_2, they are closer to Field_1 than the drones on Field_4. 

That leaves Field_4 with its current two drones (Drone_2 and Drone_3) even though it needs three; this is a “partial” protection. In our situation the highest threats (Field_3 and Field_1) get full protection, while the remaining drone capability falls on Field_4. Field_2, having the lowest threat, is left without any protection. 

This grouping follows the rules:
• Always keep the drones that are already on a field already fully protected (here Field_3 would be complete if Drone_7 reaches, and we wish to avoid disturbing Field_4 too much).
• Use the drones closest to the fields that need protection;
• Reassign from low‐priority fields (in this case Field_2) when overall resources are short.

Thus, the final assignment is as shown above.
